(LST-32 — HISTORICAL, 0.4.14 and earlier. On those manuals, printing a
 list was recursive and cycle-safe: a list that contains itself was
 capped at a depth of 64, the over-deep subtree printed as `...`, the
 error flag was set, and printing unwound safely instead of overflowing
 the stack. Hand-counted: exactly 64 opening brackets and 64 closing ones.
 Also covers (then): LST-33 (the worked example of LANGUAGE.md:2431-2434 —
 note that its inline comment "(prints: [[...]] then cyclic)" is an
 abbreviation, not a transcript; see Discrepancy 6).

 STALE as of 0.4.15 (#111): `append ring to ring.` no longer builds a
 self-referential list at all — #111 makes nesting always copy, so this
 exact program now prints `[[]]` and never reaches the depth cap. LST-32's
 ledger row is narrowed (the cap itself is still real, LANGUAGE.md:2520-
 2523, but only reachable via 64 explicit nested literal levels now); this
 probe is retained as the historical record of the pre-#111 behavior, not
 as a currently-passing claim. LST-33 is withdrawn. See
 `nested-selfappend.vox` in the step-04 probe set for the 0.4.15 behavior.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST-32.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   [[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[[...]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]]
   cyclic
)
a list called ring is [].
append ring to ring.
print ring.
on error print "cyclic".
